<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rochester, Kent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durobrivae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rochester is a town in Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where the Roman road of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">River Medway</a>. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement and then the Roman town of Durobrivae, it became in 604 the seat of England's second-oldest bishopric. Its Norman castle keep and cathedral survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rochester-kent.jpg" alt="Rochester, Kent" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rochester Cathedral west facade with Norman towers viewed from Boley Hill and Epaul ClemRutter; derivative work by SilkTork · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2006SweepsCath1crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
